From 6b4338416a56f510dc9fe9382b23901923ffbb29 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Marius Gehrmann <marius@gehrmann.solutions>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:15:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 16/22] drm: apple: Let the T8122 internal pipe drive a Type-C
 display, lid closed

With the lid closed and two external displays, macOS drives the second
one with the internal DCP's pipe (IOMFBIntDcpUsedForExtWhenLidClose):
the panel is off, and the pipe's first DPTX core is free for the
left-front USB-C port. dcpext keeps the display it drives. When the lid
opens, the pipe goes back to the panel and that display waits again.

Do the same on J613, where the internal pipe has a Type-C route to that
port (dcp->clamshell):

Fabric. The port has two pipelines. The internal one counts as busy
unless the lid is closed and the panel off (dcp->clamshell_free), and it
ranks after dcpext, as a hybrid whose fixed output is the panel. So a
free dcpext always takes the display, lid open or closed, and the
internal pipe only takes one that would otherwise wait. When it becomes
free it takes such a waiting display, as any pipeline that is let go of
(dcp_typec_pipeline_changed()). When the lid opens, that display lets
go of it (powered off, HPD dropped, display released, route taken down)
and goes to dcpext if dcpext is free by then, or waits again with the
usual log line. If dcpext becomes free while the internal pipe drives
the display, nothing moves until the lid opens.

Lid. The lid work (previous patch) also decides whether the pipe is
free: after A475(2), and once the panel is off. As macOS opens the lid,
the display lets go of the pipe first, then IOMFB is told. Powering the
panel off or on with its CRTC re-runs it.

Session. Once the panel has started, the DPTX endpoint is started for
the route, and the DPAV controller endpoint with it, as for the T6030
external processors: the dev series had it started in every test, and
the firmware only sent reports there (type 0xc0), never a call that
would need an answer; leaving it stopped was never tested. The DPTX
RemotePort unit 0 (core 0, crossbar dispext0,0) is DPTX port 0 as
usual; the panel's own DPTX port, if announced, is never bound. The
link is connected as on the T8122 dcpext (AsahiLinux/linux#607 model,
dcp_ext_dptx_607()), the same firmware image. Call 25, which the
firmware sends to each RemotePort when the lid state changes (its DPTX
controllers' setActive()), is acknowledged quietly. While the route is
active (v14->typec), the session handles the display description as
on an external processor: the published timings become the pipe's
modes, and the display is reported connected once described. Its mode
is set on the 14.7 session with A472(1) before A411 (A411 first leaves
the firmware with "Modeset done, but pipe not enabled"); its CRTC off
powers it off (A472(0)), as on an external processor, and the usual
Type-C DPMS path releases and restores the link. Swaps are sized to its
mode, without notch rows or the panel's colour matrix, and a refused
swap does not end the session. When the route is taken down, the
panel's one mode is the pipe's catalog again.

Connectors. While the display has the pipe, and until the lid opens,
the panel's connector is reported disconnected: compositors such as
aquamarine keep a CRTC for every connected connector, and the pipe has
one. It comes back once the compositor switched the CRTC off after the
lid opened, or two seconds later.

Tested on J613 in the dev series with the same firmware calls, with a
display on each port and the lid closed and opened six times: the
second display came up on the internal pipe each time (first frame
about 28 ms after the mode set), the panel came back each time, no
kernel errors. The dcpext-first rule, the order A472(1) before A411,
the give-back through the fabric and the connector handling are new.

Other machines are unchanged, and so is J613 without the route.

